  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 PM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nThere is a low-end (10-15%) chance for a few isolated showers to \ndevelop across east-central WI this evening as a mid-level warm \nfront lifts northward. Otherwise expect dry and quiet conditions for \nthe rest of the day. Saturday strong warm advection and deep mixing \nwill bring unseasonably warm and windy conditions to the region. \nHigh temperatures are forecast to reach the low 80s across most of \nthe region except along the lakeshore where highs will be capped in \nthe low 70s. Southerly winds will increase Saturday morning with \nsustained winds reaching 15-20 mph during the afternoon and wind \ngusts up to 30-35 mph. As the PBL decouples Saturday evening and \nthe pressure gradiant begins to slack off winds will gradually \nweaken Saturday night into Sunday. While Sunday won't be as breezy\nthe warmth will stick around with highs in the upper 70s to \nmiddle 80s, warmest in central WI.\n\nMonday will see the next chance for area wide precipitation with \nNBM probs showing a 50-60% chance for rain Monday afternoon and \nevening as a cold front pushes across the region. Depending on \ntiming of the cold front there is a low-end chance (~5%) for a few\nisolated thunderstorms during the afternoon as weak instability \nbuilds ahead of the front. However, at this point the risk for \nsevere storms is very low. QPF looks limited with this event as \nmedium ranged ensembles show less than a 30-40% chance for greater\nthan 0.1 inches of rain through Tuesday morning across the \nforecast area. Another more widespread chance for precipitation \nmay comes during the middle to end of next week as global \nensembles show a deepening low pressure system lifting across the \nwestern Great Lakes. Still a lot of uncertainty with the details \nof this system, but this looks like the best chance for a soaking \nrain over the next 7-10 days.",
